- Discusses durability risks, latency impacts, and operational complexity
- Emphasizes the boundary between A and B as the architectural core
- Discusses what happens if the boundary becomes ""unscharf"" (blurry)
- Provides 5 key justifications at the end

**Comparison:**

| Aspect | Model | Golden |
|--------|-------|--------|
| Coverage of all 3 mods | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes |
| Quantitative tradeoffs | ✅ Matrix provided | ⚠️ Qualitative only |
| Risk assessment | ✅ Clear | ✅ Clear |
| Architectural insight | ✅ Good | ✅ Excellent (boundary emphasis) |
| Depth of reasoning | ✅ Good | ✅ Excellent (5 justifications) |

**Advantage: Golden Standard** — The emphasis on the boundary between pre-commit and post-commit work as the *architectural core* is a deeper insight. The model treats this as a design pattern; the golden standard treats it as the fundamental principle.

